# Eponym common library: phase-indexed handles / typestate (issue #481).
#
# A handle indexed by its phase: the type of a socket records which phase
# it is in, and every operation's type states the phase it requires and
# the phase it delivers. Misuse is a type error, not a runtime failure:
# send-before-connect, use-after-close, and double-close are all rejected
# at elaboration time (see examples/ep/28*-neg.ep).
#
# The operations are postulates (mock socket ops); a real library would
# bind them to socket code. Linear (1) binders ensure each handle is used
# exactly once along a path (Issue #66), so a handle cannot be dropped
# silently or used twice within checked code.
#
# Honest boundary (see docs/known-limitations.md, "Static checks do not
# manage Nim resources"): Eponym checks phase ordering inside checked
# terms. It cannot see Nim-side aliasing: two Nim references to the same
# socket are invisible to it. This library demonstrates checking at the
# eponym API boundary; it does not claim end-to-end resource management.
import "nat.ep"

# Phases of a socket's lifecycle.
data(Phase, Univ) where
| unconnP : Phase
| connP : Phase
| closedP : Phase

# A socket indexed by its current phase.
data(Sock, Phase -> Univ) where
| mkSock (p : Phase) : Sock p

# Lifecycle operations. Each consumes the handle in its required phase
# and delivers it in the next phase; the index change is the transition.
postulate(connectH, Pi(1 s: Sock unconnP, Sock connP))
postulate(sendH, Pi(x: Nat, Pi(1 s: Sock connP, Sock connP)))
postulate(recvH, Pi(1 s: Sock connP, Sig(x: Nat, Sock connP)))
postulate(closeH, Pi(1 s: Sock connP, Sock closedP))
